\ul{
\li{\strong{Warm.} The proposed line is where today's line is (0.88-1.04 with 8 workers, 0.92-1.03 with one): no steady-state gain can be claimed, as section 2 predicts. It reaches that state two passes earlier: its pass 3 is steady on every corpus (Init 0.84, Std 1.41, cedar 2.04, reachability 1.70 s) where today's line needs five or six (Init 1.41, 0.99, 0.91, 0.86; cedar 2.95, 2.42, 2.27, 2.19).}
\li{\strong{Cold.} 14-48 \% less than today below Mathlib (Init 4.14 -> 3.57 s in this window; 5.30 -> 2.87 and 4.74 -> 2.83 s in windows 7 and 3; Std 5.45 -> 3.11; cedar 9.9 -> 5.15; reachability 3.95 -> 2.54), 12 \% on the first pass of Mathlib (31.0 -> 27.2 s). The native image is still 1.75-3 times faster cold on Init, Std and cedar; on reachability and on Mathlib the proposed JVM line now starts as fast as the image (2.54 against 2.63 s; 27.2 against 29.1 s).}
\li{\strong{JVM against native image, warm, same jars, same windows:} 0.68-0.88 times the image's wall clock with 8 workers on all five corpora, 0.67-0.87 with one worker. \code{\verb~|work/status/warm.md~} had 0.71-0.91: unchanged with today's jars (the image's Mathlib pass is 28.3 s here, 35.3 s there).}
\li{\strong{Mathlib.} One pair here (24.91 against 23.92 s) reads as 4 \% against the proposed line; the same flags gave 22.56 and 21.87 s in window 9, today's line 23.94 s in window 6: the pass varies by +-6 \% between windows, and the A B A B of window 9 (section 2.2) is the comparison to trust: huge pages -6 \%, the cap nothing.}
\li{\strong{Memory.} Below Mathlib the JVM needs 1.6-2.5 times the image's resident set with either line (its young generation of 1 GB is touched in full). On Mathlib the 7 GB cap brings it from 8.4 GB to 6.1 GB here (5.7-6.7 GB over all runs; image 4.2 GB).}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. Findings for the machine's owners (no module was edited)}

\p{\strong{J1-F1 (M6, \code{\verb~|Machine.heapAllows~} / \code{\verb~|YOUNG_RESERVE~}): the young reserve is a third of the heap whatever \code{\verb~|-Xmn~} says, and that is what sets the machine's memory floor.} \code{\verb~|YOUNG_RESERVE = Runtime.maxMemory() / 3~}; both launchers run with \code{\verb~|-Xmn1g~}. On Mathlib with \code{\verb~|-Xmx6g -Xmn1g~} the machine refuses 2 session growths in the first pass and 6 in the second: \code{\verb~|a session array of 33554432 bytes does not fit (2375316784 bytes free of 6335102976, reserve 268435456)~}, i.e. 2 x 32 MB + 256 MB + 2,112 MB = 2,432 MB wanted against 2,375 MB free, where the real young generation is 1,024 MB; before each refusal it calls \code{\verb~|System.gc()~} (28 full collections in two passes; with 13 GB: none). Reading the reserve from the VM (\code{\verb~|MemoryPoolMXBean~} of the young pools, or the \code{\verb~|NewSize~} / \code{\verb~|MaxNewSize~} flags through \code{\verb~|HotSpotDiagnosticMXBean~}) instead of assuming \code{\verb~|NewRatio~} would make 6 GB a clean cap: 5.8 GB resident in every pass at the wall clock of 13 GB (section 4). Evidence: \code{\verb~|work/j1/logs/w08-mathlib-base-x6g.{out,declines.txt,gc.log,stats.json}~}.}

\p{\strong{J1-F2 (machine owners; the convention H10): C2 compiles all of it, and its inlining budget is the only thing it runs into; neither a flag nor a reshaping for the JIT is worth doing.} Evidence in section 2.1 (which methods, which messages). \code{\verb~|Infer.piLoop~}, \code{\verb~|Conv.bindLoop~}, \code{\verb~|Conv.convLoop~}, \code{\verb~|MachineSession.run~}, \code{\verb~|Infer.twY~} (cedar), \code{\verb~|Infer.twalk~}, \code{\verb~|Infer.k_ck1/4/5~}, \code{\verb~|Cargs.cargsFrom~} (Init) end at 8,900-11,200 inlined bytecodes and 18,000-18,900 nodes; C2 answers "NodeCountInliningCutoff", "size > DesiredMethodLimit", "inlining too deep" for call sites with 10^6-10^8 profile counts, among them \code{\verb~|Conv.convLoop -> convCore1~} and \code{\verb~|Terms.kind -> Terms.arr~} (292 M in \code{\verb~|Infer.piLoop~}). The flags that move those limits (\code{\verb~|MaxInlineLevel~} 9 / 25, \code{\verb~|MaxInlineSize=100~}, \code{\verb~|FreqInlineSize=600~}, \code{\verb~|InlineSmallCode~} 1,000 / 6,000, \code{\verb~|-ClipInlining~}) change the warm pass by nothing that repeats (section 2.2). So the source-level equivalents - folding the private three-way select \code{\verb~|Terms.arr~} into \code{\verb~|kind~} / \code{\verb~|a~} / \code{\verb~|b~} / \code{\verb~|fvMask~} / \code{\verb~|word~} (one inline level less at the hottest leaf), splitting the arms of \code{\verb~|Eval.atom~}, shielding entry steps with \code{\verb~|dontinline~} - should not be expected to pay either; \code{\verb~|h10/hotspot-inline.cmd~} measured nothing on C2 before (NOTES-M6 F23) and this agrees. What does show up in the profile is memory: the hottest frames are probes into session tables (\code{\verb~|Runs.sameKept~} 8.3 \%, \code{\verb~|Values.closureAt~} 6.3 \%, \code{\verb~|Values.neu~} 4.8 \%, \code{\verb~|Values.closureProbe~} 3.7 \%), and huge pages are the one flag with a consistent gain. A change that makes those probes fewer or denser will help more than any flag.}

\p{\strong{J1-F3 (machine owners, M8): a mature profile gives C2 worse code for the machine than a young one.} With the AOT cache the VM compiles from the profile of four training passes: 25 uncommon traps in a whole Init run instead of 246, 3.43 MB of C2 code instead of 2.65 MB, 25 compile units at the inlining budget instead of 12, and every later pass 5-9 \% slower (section 3.3). The step methods have many rarely-taken arms (\code{\verb~|*Slow~}, \code{\verb~|sig*~}, \code{\verb~|spill*~}, \code{\verb~|fault~}, the cold switch arms); C2's own pruning of branches it has not seen is doing useful work, and whatever keeps rare arms out of the compile units (they are already out of line; the test in front of them is what the profile sees) keeps the units inside the budget.}

\p{\strong{J1-F4 (rlvm-driver / rlvm-main, P7 and the integrator): the resident set of a machine run is decided by accident.} The only full collections of a machine run are the \code{\verb~|System.gc()~} calls of \code{\verb~|Machine.heapAllows~}, made when a pooled session array of 32 MB or more grows; between them the serial collector lets the old generation fill. So the same flags give 5.7-6.7 GB after the first pass of Mathlib under a 7 GB cap (seven runs), 8.0-8.7 GB under 13 GB (\code{\verb~|warm.md~}: 11.5 GB after three passes), and in a later pass, where the sessions no longer grow, the cap itself. If a bound on the resident set is wanted independently of \code{\verb~|-Xmx~}, the driver's \code{\verb~|gc-after-mib~} is the existing lever; it was not measured here.}

\p{\strong{J1-F5 (rlvm-export, LP2; small): the reader's block loop stays in C1 code.} \code{\verb~|ExportReader.sequential~} runs once per load with about 20,000 iterations on cedar, so it never reaches the tier-4 back-edge threshold: after five passes it still runs as tier-3 OSR code (with full profiling). Its body is calls into C2 code, so the cost is small; \code{\verb~|Worker.run~} (the worker's unit loop, 1,092 bytecodes) reached tier 4 only in pass 5 for the same reason. Nothing to do unless a profile of the loader shows the shell.}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. What was not established}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Effects below about 5 \%.} The host was shared for the whole session (load average 20-60); two base rows of one window differ by up to 5 \% with 8 workers. Every "nothing" above means "nothing above that".}
\li{\strong{Why exactly the cached profile costs 5-9 \%}: the larger compile units are an observation on one diagnostic pair (Init), not a proof of cause.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|LoopUnrollLimit~}, \code{\verb~|MaxNodeLimit~}, \code{\verb~|-DontCompileHugeMethods~}} were not timed (nothing for them to act on, section 2.1); tier thresholds were tried once (a fifth of the tier-4 defaults: nothing, section 2.2), higher ones not.}
\li{\strong{One worker on Mathlib} (about 190 s per pass) and \strong{Graal on Mathlib} were not run.}
\li{\strong{The reference engine} was not measured with the proposed flags; its heap line is unchanged.}
\li{\strong{Huge pages elsewhere}: this host has transparent huge pages in \code{\verb~|madvise~} mode, where the flag takes effect. With \code{\verb~|never~} the VM is expected to print one warning and run as today, with \code{\verb~|always~} the flag changes nothing; neither was tested (the setting is the host's, also for a container).}
\li{\strong{x86}: every number here is aarch64 (Cortex-X925). The arena's runner is x86; the JVM launcher is not what the arena runs.}
\li{\strong{A smaller young generation} on Mathlib (\code{\verb~|-Xmn512m~}) and the 6 GB cap after a fix of J1-F1.}
\li{\strong{A cache trained on Init} (between init-prelude, which is not enough, and Std, which is).}
}
}

